From 3270470b3a3edcb708593d73b34a4265487d507d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 22:07:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Syscalls: return EFAULT for bad user pointers in FEX's own
 handlers

What Linux does: every user pointer a system call reads or writes goes through copy_from_user/copy_to_user,
get_user/put_user or strncpy_from_user; a fault there becomes -EFAULT and the caller carries on.

What FEX did: passthrough syscalls hand the pointer to the host kernel and behave the same, but the handlers that
build the x86-64 view themselves dereference guest pointers from host C++ code. A bad pointer killed the process
with SIGSEGV inside FEX: rt_sigaction, rt_sigprocmask, rt_sigpending, sigaltstack, uname, stat/lstat/fstat/
newfstatat, arch_prctl(ARCH_GET_FS/GS), every path FEX parses itself (open, openat, access, faccessat, faccessat2,
readlink, readlinkat, the stat family, execve/execveat with their argv/envp), and seccomp(SET_MODE_FILTER) read the
program before validating it. The fault-safe accessors upstream added for this (FaultSafeUserMemAccess::CopyFromUser/
CopyToUser with a PC-range fault fixup) existed, but the SIGSEGV path deliberately died after the fixup
("intentionally stub-implemented", unittests Known_Failures).

Fix:
- HandleFrontendSIGSEGV returns to the accessor with EFAULT instead of dying after the fixup.
- New FaultSafeUserMemAccess::IsStringReadable (same fixup scheme) for guest path/argv strings.
- The handlers above copy their user structures in and out through CopyFromUser/CopyToUser around the unchanged
  internals, in Linux's order: input copied before anything changes, output copied afterwards (-EFAULT then, with
  the change already made, as on Linux). readlink of /proc/self/exe also stops NUL-padding the buffer (Linux copies
  exactly the link length). Release-build VerifyIsWritable() in fstat/stat no longer aborts on a NULL buffer.
Cost: the copies are a few dozen bytes on syscalls that are already emulated in C++; a path string is walked once
more before FEX parses it. Nothing changes for passthrough syscalls or for JIT code.

Tests: eac-tests/syscall-efault (51 cases, each in a forked child) and the upstream unittests syscalls_efault.cpp,
extended with the cases above; syscalls_efault.64 leaves Known_Failures (not run here: no x86-64 sysroot in the
build guest). Native x86-64 (kernel 6.19): PASS. Before: 26 cases killed by SIGSEGV, seccomp case -EINVAL. After:
PASS; eac-tests/run-all.sh 23/23 as expected.

Limits: the 32-bit (x32/) handlers are not converted (syscalls_efault.32 stays a known failure); ioctl and other
emulated structures not listed above are unchanged. A pointer that another thread unmaps between the string check
and the host syscall can still fault inside FEX.

Upstream: issue #3942 (open; FaultSafeUserMemAccess and the Known_Failures entry are unchanged on FEX main as
triaged at 14c9268).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
